  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe propose a multi-stage optimization approach that combines spectral regularization, randomized restarts, and adaptive grid refinement to address the non-convexity of the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. Key improvements include:\n1. Treating the overlap constraint in Fourier domain to enforce global correlation properties\n2. Progressive grid refinement (n=500 \u2192 1000 \u2192 2000) to maintain solution quality while reducing local minima\n3. Stochastic gradient projection with temperature-based acceptance to enable escaping shallow local optima\n4. Exploiting the problem's symmetry through Fourier transforms to guide search\n\nThe central idea is to use smooth function approximation (log-sum-exp) of the maximum overlap integral, balanced with spectral constraints that enforce the functional properties. This differs from previous methods by:\n- Using Fourier-filtered restarts to inject structural diversity\n- Implementing anisotropic rounding in the frequency domain\n- Adapting the smoothness parameter dynamically\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe optimize h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] minimizing:\n- Smoothed objective: S(h) = (1/\u03b1) log(\u03a3 exp(-\u03b1 \u2022 C_k)) where C_k = integral h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Spectral constraints: |Fourier coefficients| \u2265 \u03b8 \u2022 |S(f)| (appropriate frequency band)\n- Integral normalization: \u222bh(x)dx = 2 \u2022 (n/2)/n (normalized)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   Use Chebyshev expansion on Fourier-filtered Fourier coefficients\n   `h0 = inverse_fourier(fixed_spectral_floor(fourier(U(0,1orted noise)))`\n\n2. Global optimization loop:\n  while budget > 100s & improvement > 1e-8\n      alpha *= 0.8   # adjust strong smoothing\n      grid_coarsening = gamma * exp(-iter/time_scale)  # adaptive grid\n      h = L_BFGSB(h, obj=spectral_approx, grad=num_gradient, \n                  constraints=[bounds, integral])\n      h = frequency_informed_adaptive_rounding(h)\n\n3. Local refinement:\n   Use stochastic acceptance: \n      for i,j in random_pairs:\n          trial = flip_h_ij(h)\n          r = exp((obj(trial) - obj(h))/alpha)\n          accept = (np.random() < min(1,r))\n          h[i], h[j] = trial[i], trial[j] (conditionally)\n\n   This maintains diversity while focusing on improvement\n\n4. Cross-scale transfer:\n   Use interpolation maps to transfer between n=500, 1000, 2000 grids\n\nSearch choices:\n- Primary frequency band: 0.1* f_max to 1.0*f_max\n- Temperature schedule: T=1e2 exp(-t/1e3)\n- Grid scaling: n=500,1000,2000 (time budget: \u221d n^2)\n\nBudget and robustness:\n- Triscale optimization: 33% each stage, checking feasibility at 50s intervals\n- Cooldown: save best solution every 500 steps\n- Recovery: try lower grid at poor progress\n\nValidation:\n- Feasibility: clip_to_bounds(np.clip(project_feasible(h,0.0,1.0, n))\n- Normalization: scale_integral(h, target=1)\n- Objective: S_smooth(h) = (1/\u03b1) log(\u03a3 exp(-\u03b1 \u2022 (correlation_full[idx]))))\n- Early termination: if improvement<1e-8 for 10 steps\n\nReturn contract:\nReturns (h, C5, n) with:\n- h: array satisfying 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and sum(h)\u2248n/2\n- C5 = max_k (dx \u2022 correlation_full[k])\n- n: grid size\n\nKey innovations over previous:\n1. Natural gradient with respect to Fourier coefficients\n2. Stochastic projection solver for large grids\n3. Dual-space optimization (frequency + time)\n4. Adaptive cooperativity parameter",
